{"author":{"id":"a66e0db0201c515c5090d5e52c036411dda9ba2f138110a926616a18a8ea0dd6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790978405,"nonce":"pcqKZ4AXSa+JMpOSWHEpg9xvrzE=","title":"awaiting device.lost() after device.destroy() hangs on the web: destroy is never sent","message":"In web.rs, `device_destroy` encodes `GPUDevice.destroy` and releases the device and queue, but leaves them in the batch. A program that then awaits a future settled by the destroy, such as `device.lost()`, waits forever. The future settles only when the agent runs the batch, and an await sends nothing.\n\nReproduced 2026-10-02 in headless Chrome through Ash's page, with x-idl's generated `lost` forward: `var lost = device.lost(); device.destroy(); lost.await();` never returns. With any call that sends the batch between the destroy and the await (`texture.width()`), it resolves with reason Destroyed and message \"Device was destroyed.\", and the program exits 0.\n\nThe general case: any command whose effect a pending promise depends on must be sent before the program can block on that promise. Sending the batch in device_destroy covers this one. Batching in front of an await (Ash's ash_host_watch hook runs on the program's thread) would cover all of them.","files":null}]}